---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { layerLabel } from "../../data/portfolio";
import type { Language } from "../../types";

interface Props {
	lang: Language;
}

const { lang } = Astro.props;
const en = lang === "en";
const base = en ? "" : "/fr";

const t = en
	? {
			title: "MangaReader: Self-Hosted Manga Library | Yanis Amani",
			description: "A self-hosted manga library in Rust: multi-source sync, series merging, accounts and a fast web reader.",
			tagline: "A manga reader that scrapes chapters from third-party sites, merges them into series, and serves them through a web interface.",
			github: "View on GitHub",
			techLabel: "Tech Stack",
			prev: "← Previous project",
			next: "Next project →",
		}
	: {
			title: "MangaReader : bibliothèque de manga | Yanis Amani",
			description: "Une bibliothèque de manga auto-hébergée en Rust : sync multi-sources, fusion de séries, comptes et lecteur web rapide.",
			tagline: "Un lecteur de manga qui scrape des chapitres depuis des sites tiers, les fusionne en séries, et les sert via une interface web.",
			github: "Voir sur GitHub",
			techLabel: "Stack Technique",
			prev: "← Projet précédent",
			next: "Projet suivant →",
		};

const mermaidSource = `flowchart TB
    CLI[ScraperEngine] --> DB[(SQLite)]
    WEB[Axum Server] --> DB
    WEB <--> BROWSER[Browser]`;

const breadcrumbs = [
	{ name: en ? "Home" : "Accueil", path: en ? "/" : "/fr" },
	{ name: en ? "Projects" : "Projets", path: `${base}/#projects` },
	{ name: "MangaReader", path: `${base}/projects/mangaread` },
];
---

<Layout title={t.title} description={t.description} lang={lang} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} index>
	<article class="project-page" data-animate>
		<div class="container">
			<header class="project-head">
				<p class="project-layer">{layerLabel("product", lang)}</p>
				<h1 class="project-title">MangaReader</h1>
				<p class="project-tagline">{t.tagline}</p>
			</header>

			<div class="project-layout">
				<aside class="project-meta">
					<div class="project-links">
						<a href="https://github.com/DevYatsu/mangareader" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn-primary">{t.github}</a>
					</div>
					<div class="project-meta__block">
						<span class="tech-label">{t.techLabel}</span>
						<div class="tech-list">
							<a href="https://rust-lang.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="tech-tag">Rust</a>
							<a href="https://docs.rs/axum" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="tech-tag">Axum</a>
							<a href="https://sqlite.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="tech-tag">SQLite</a>
							<a href="https://docker.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="tech-tag">Docker</a>
							<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="tech-tag">HTML/CSS/JS</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</aside>

				<div class="project-content">
				{en ? (
					<Fragment>
						<h2>What it does</h2>
						<p>MangaReader is a self-hosted manga library. It fetches chapters from third-party sites, merges the same title into one series, and serves a reader with accounts, bookmarks and synced progress. New chapters sync in the background.</p>

						<h2>Architecture</h2>
						<p>Three Rust crates in one workspace:</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>mangaread-core</strong>: domain types, SQLite repository, series merging logic</li>
							<li><strong>scraper</strong>: SiteAdapter trait with per-site implementations (Demonicscans, Manhuaplus), CLI binary + library</li>
							<li><strong>website</strong>: Axum web server with <a href="https://maud.lambda.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maud</a> templates, accounts, an admin area and static assets</li>
						</ul>
						<p>The database uses <a href="https://sqlite.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SQLite</a> in WAL mode, in a single file.</p>
						<p>Series from different sources are merged by normalized title.</p>

						<pre class="mermaid">{mermaidSource}</pre>

						<p><strong>Accounts are optional.</strong> Visitors can read without signing in, and their progress stays in the browser. Signed-in readers get progress, bookmarks and notes synced across devices.</p>

						<h2>Screenshots</h2>
						<div class="screenshot-grid">
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/home.jpg" alt="MangaReader home page" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Browse your series library</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/series.jpg" alt="MangaReader series view" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>View chapters and track reading</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/series-details.jpg" alt="MangaReader series details" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Manage and organize</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/chapter.jpg" alt="MangaReader chapter view" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Chapter viewing interface</figcaption>
							</figure>
						</div>

						<h2>The hard parts</h2>
						<p><strong>Chromedriver.</strong> The scraper uses a real browser for JavaScript-heavy manga sites. Getting chromedriver to behave in Docker was 30% of the dev time. Headless mode bugs, sandbox issues, zombie processes: the usual webdriver circus.</p>
						<p><strong><a href="https://sqlite.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SQLite</a> and threading.</strong> <a href="https://docs.rs/rusqlite" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rusqlite</a>'s Connection isn't Sync, so each scraper thread opens its own connection. The main AppState wraps its own in a <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mutex</a>. Sounds simple, but the borrow checker had opinions.</p>

						<p class="pull-quote">Fighting the borrow checker for the 50th time at 2am is a rite of passage.</p>

						<h2>What I learned</h2>
						<p>Splitting this <a href="https://www.rust-lang.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rust</a> project into workspace crates taught me more than any tutorial. Rust's type system is genuinely great for domain modeling (no invalid states possible), but fighting the borrow checker for the 50th time at 2am is a rite of passage.</p>
						<p>The scraper adapter pattern (trait + per-site implementations) paid off immediately when adding a second source. And the sync loop (background thread, 6-hour interval, parallel scraping) taught me about <a href="https://tokio.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tokio</a> channels and graceful shutdown the hard way.</p>
						<p>Also: always pin your chromedriver version. Always.</p>
					</Fragment>
				) : (
					<Fragment>
						<h2>Ce que ça fait</h2>
						<p>MangaReader est une bibliothèque de manga auto-hébergée. Elle récupère des chapitres sur des sites tiers, fusionne un même titre en une série, et sert un lecteur avec comptes, marque-pages et progression synchronisée. Les nouveaux chapitres se synchronisent en arrière-plan.</p>

						<h2>Architecture</h2>
						<p>Trois crates Rust dans un workspace :</p>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>mangaread-core</strong>: types du domaine, repository SQLite, logique de fusion des séries</li>
							<li><strong>scraper</strong>: trait SiteAdapter avec implémentations par site (Demonicscans, Manhuaplus), binaire CLI + bibliothèque</li>
							<li><strong>website</strong>: serveur web Axum avec templates <a href="https://maud.lambda.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maud</a>, comptes, espace admin et assets statiques</li>
						</ul>
						<p>La base de données utilise <a href="https://sqlite.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SQLite</a> en mode WAL, dans un seul fichier.</p>

						<pre class="mermaid">{mermaidSource}</pre>

						<p><strong>Comptes optionnels.</strong> Les visiteurs lisent sans se connecter et leur progression reste dans le navigateur. Les lecteurs connectés retrouvent progression, marque-pages et notes sur tous leurs appareils.</p>

						<h2>Captures d'écran</h2>
						<div class="screenshot-grid">
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/home.jpg" alt="Page d'accueil MangaReader" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Parcourir votre bibliothèque</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/series.jpg" alt="Vue série MangaReader" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Voir les chapitres</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/series-details.jpg" alt="Détails série MangaReader" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Gérer et organiser</figcaption>
							</figure>
							<figure class="screenshot">
								<img src="/mangareader-images/chapter.jpg" alt="Lecture chapitre MangaReader" loading="lazy" width="400" height="248" />
								<figcaption>Interface de lecture</figcaption>
							</figure>
						</div>

						<h2>Les parties difficiles</h2>
						<p><strong>Chromedriver.</strong> Le scraper utilise un vrai navigateur pour les sites manga chargés en JS. Faire marcher chromedriver dans Docker a pris 30% du temps de dev. Bugs de mode headless, problèmes de sandbox, processus zombies : le cirque habituel des webdrivers.</p>
						<p><strong><a href="https://sqlite.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SQLite</a> et le threading.</strong> La Connection de <a href="https://docs.rs/rusqlite" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rusqlite</a> n'est pas Sync, donc chaque thread de scraping ouvre sa propre connexion. Le AppState principal wrappe la sienne dans un <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mutex</a>. Ça semble simple, mais le borrow checker avait des opinions.</p>

						<p class="pull-quote">Se battre avec le borrow checker pour la 50ème fois à 2h du matin, c'est un rite de passage.</p>

						<h2>Ce que j'ai appris</h2>
						<p>Découper ce projet <a href="https://www.rust-lang.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rust</a> en crates de workspace m'a plus appris que n'importe quel tutoriel. Le système de types de Rust est génial pour la modélisation du domaine (pas d'états invalides possibles), mais se battre avec le borrow checker pour la 50ème fois à 2h du matin, c'est un rite de passage.</p>
						<p>Le pattern d'adaptateur (trait + implémentations par site) a payé immédiatement en ajoutant une deuxième source. Et la boucle de sync (thread background, intervalle de 6h, scraping parallèle) m'a appris les channels <a href="https://tokio.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tokio</a> et l'arrêt gracieux à la dure.</p>
					</Fragment>
				)}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<nav class="project-nav">
		<div class="container project-nav-inner">
			<a href={`${base}/projects/yatsuscript`} class="btn-ghost">{t.prev}</a>
			<a href={`${base}/projects/ghaiari`} class="btn-ghost">{t.next}</a>
		</div>
	</nav>
</Layout>
